. Maybe the service was never created, or it\'s named differently. Let me check the deployments and see if there\'s any mention of a service. \n\nWait, the deployment\'s YAML might have a selector that matches the pods, but if the service isn\'t created, the pods are isolated. The user could have a service that\'s not named fraud-detection. Maybe it\'s using a different name, like the same as the deployment or something else. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the service was deleted or never created. To confirm, I should check all services in the namespace. Using `kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop` would list all existing services. If fraud-detection isn\'t there, then the service is missing. \n\nAlso, checking the service account or any related resources might help. But first, let\'s list all services to see if there\'s any clue. If there\'s no service, the root cause is that the service wasn\'t created, possibly due to a misconfiguration in the Helm chart or deployment. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to list all services in the namespace to confirm the absence of the fraud-detection service.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nThe `fraud-detection` service is not found, which explains why there are no calls to it in the dependency graph. To confirm this, I will list all services in the `astronomy-shop` namespace to verify the absence of the service and identify any potential misconfigurations or missing resources. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=495, prompt_tokens=4391, total_tokens=4886, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe92-f4d9-7562-80a3-13a73e790676